<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Inflated by ui/player/VideoSurface.kt instead of constructing PlayerView(ctx) directly, to
     select the video-only content layout - no programmatic setter for it, only this XML attribute.

     surface_view (also Media3's default): SurfaceFlinger composites the decoded frames as their own
     layer behind the window, usually on a hardware overlay. A texture_view, used here before, is
     drawn by the app's RenderThread, so every decoded frame redrew the whole window - measured on a
     Mi A2 while a 720p channel just played inline: about 85 window frames a second, the RenderThread
     at 60% of a core and the app at 1.3 cores, against 0-3 frames, almost no RenderThread time and
     less than half the app CPU with this one (fullscreen and the mini bar alike).

     Two limits of a SurfaceView the UI has to respect:
     - It cannot follow a graphics layer above it (alpha, scale). The player's opening transform
       covers it meanwhile; see ui/components/OpenTransform.kt.
     - Window content around it must stay opaque: wherever the window is translucent, the video
       layer behind it shows through. Content drawn over it (controls, scrims, the tips spotlight)
       composites over the video normally.

     It was once switched to texture_view because Compose's Play/Pause button over the video seemed
     to lose its tap to the enclosing "toggle controls" click. Touch dispatch does not depend on the
     surface type: Compose hit-tests its own nodes before the AndroidView, and the button was
     re-verified on a Mi A2 with this surface (tap pauses, controls stay). -->
<androidx.media3.ui.PlayerView
    xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:app="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res-auto"
    android:id="@+id/player_view"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    app:player_layout_id="@layout/player_surface_content"
    app:surface_type="surface_view"
    app:use_controller="false" />
